import Applied.Statistics.SPRTWaldIID

/-!
# Wald's sharper SPRT error bounds `α ≤ (1-β)/A` and `β ≤ B(1-α)` (change of measure)

- id: stat-sprt-wald-change-of-measure
- area: statistics
- status: formalized
- source: A. Wald, "Sequential tests of statistical hypotheses", Ann. Math. Statist. 16 (1945); A. Wald, "Sequential Analysis" (1947), Section 3 (the inequalities `A ≤ (1-β)/α`, `B ≥ β/(1-α)`; exact section and equation numbers to be confirmed). The change-of-measure proof on the events `{τ = n, Λ n ≥ A}` is the standard textbook argument (e.g. Siegmund, "Sequential Analysis", 1985, Ch. 2; to be confirmed).
- used-in: sequential clinical trials, quality control, sequential A/B testing
- prerequisites: lower Lebesgue integral on sets, countable additivity
- statement-review: unreviewed

Informal statement.

Let `P₀, P₁` be measures on `Ω`, `F n` (`n ∈ ℕ`) σ-algebras contained in the ambient one, `Λ n`
real random variables and `τ : Ω → ℕ∞` a random time. Hypotheses (`WaldSetup`):
(i) `{τ = n}` is `F n`-measurable; (ii) `Λ n` is `F n`-measurable; (iii) density: for every
`F n`-measurable set `S`, `P₁ S = ∫⁻_S ofReal(Λ n) dP₀` (negative values of `Λ n` count as `0`).
No monotonicity of `F` is assumed. Write `R_A = {τ < ∞, Λ τ ≥ A}` and `C_B = {τ < ∞, Λ τ ≤ B}`.

(1) For every real `A`, `ofReal A · P₀(R_A) ≤ P₁(R_A)` (`wald_upper`).
(2) For every real `B`, `P₁(C_B) ≤ ofReal B · P₀(C_B)` (`wald_lower`).
(3) If `P₁` is a probability measure and `Acc` is a measurable event disjoint from `R_A`, then
`ofReal A · P₀(R_A) ≤ 1 - P₁(Acc)`: with `α = P₀(R_A)`, `β = P₁(Acc)`, `A α ≤ 1 - β`
(`wald_alpha_le`).
(4) If `P₀` is a probability measure and `Rej` is a measurable event disjoint from `C_B`, then
`P₁(C_B) ≤ ofReal B · (1 - P₀(Rej))`: `β ≤ B (1 - α)` (`wald_beta_le`).
All quantities are in `ℝ≥0∞`; `1 - x` is truncated subtraction, harmless since the measures are
probability measures there.

(5) i.i.d. instantiation (`wald_density_iid`, `waldSetup_iid`, `sprt_alpha_le_iid`,
`sprt_beta_le_iid`). Let `ν` be a measure on the observation space `E`, `p₀, p₁ : E → ℝ`
measurable with `p₀ x > 0` and `p₁ x ≥ 0` for all `x`. Let `P₀, P₁` be probability measures on
`Ω` and `X 0, X 1, …` measurable, mutually independent under `P₀` and under `P₁`, with
`P₀.map (X i) = ν.withDensity (ofReal ∘ p₀)` and `P₁.map (X i) = ν.withDensity (ofReal ∘ p₁)`.
Let `F n = obsFiltration X n` be the σ-algebra generated by `(X 0, …, X (n-1))` and
`Λ n = ∏_{i<n} p₁(X i)/p₀(X i)`. Then (iii) holds (`wald_density_iid`, which does not use that
`P₀` is a probability measure beyond what independence implies), hence `WaldSetup` holds for
every `τ` with `{τ = n} ∈ F n`, and (3), (4) hold for this `Λ` and `F`.

Differences from the textbook statement. The textbook SPRT uses the stopping time "first exit
of `Λ` from `(B, A)`", with `α, β` its error probabilities. Here any stopping time is allowed;
the SPRT's is one instance (that it is a stopping time of `obsFiltration` is not proved
separately). In (1)–(4) the density property (iii) is an explicit hypothesis; (5) derives it
for the i.i.d. model under the extra assumption `p₀ > 0` everywhere (strict positivity of the
null density; the textbook only needs `P₁ ≪ P₀`). The theorems apply to any stopping time, not only the SPRT one,
and do not assume termination: the events include `τ < ∞` explicitly. For the SPRT, (3) with
`Acc` = acceptance event gives `α ≤ (1-β)/A`, and (4) with `Rej` = rejection event gives
`β ≤ B(1-α)`; `B < A` makes the events disjoint, but that bookkeeping is left to the user.

Not covered: null densities vanishing somewhere (`p₀ ≥ 0` with `P₁ ≪ P₀`); a proof that the
SPRT exit time is a stopping time and terminates almost surely; Wald's approximations
`A ≈ (1-β)/α`, `B ≈ β/(1-α)`; almost sure termination; expected sample size; optimality.
-/

